1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,633
Charlie Munger kept repeating one counterintuitive idea:

2
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:05,644
stop straining to be brilliant,

3
00:00:05,644 --> 00:00:07,850
and work hard at not being stupid.

4
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:10,116
Munger borrowed this mental model from

5
00:00:10,116 --> 00:00:12,800
nineteenth-century mathematician Carl Jacobi,

6
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,126
who solved complex equations with three

7
00:00:15,126 --> 00:00:15,901
famous words:

8
00:00:15,901 --> 00:00:16,318
invert,

9
00:00:16,318 --> 00:00:17,153
always invert.

10
00:00:17,153 --> 00:00:19,300
Turn every hard problem upside down.

11
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,626
Asking how to build extraordinary wealth

12
00:00:21,626 --> 00:00:22,963
produces vague guesses.

13
00:00:22,963 --> 00:00:25,464
Asking what guarantees financial ruin gives

14
00:00:25,464 --> 00:00:26,685
unmistakable answers:

15
00:00:26,685 --> 00:00:27,790
high-interest debt,

16
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:28,720
status upgrades,

17
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:29,767
zero skill margin,

18
00:00:29,767 --> 00:00:30,930
and emotional panic.

19
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:33,190
Run a Five-Trap Inversion today:

20
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:35,168
write down your top goal for

21
00:00:35,168 --> 00:00:35,804
the year,

22
00:00:35,804 --> 00:00:38,629
list the five daily behaviors guaranteed

23
00:00:38,629 --> 00:00:39,476
to wreck it,

24
00:00:39,476 --> 00:00:42,443
and build physical guardrails to eliminate

25
00:00:42,443 --> 00:00:43,220
them first.

26
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:44,464
In compounding games,

27
00:00:44,464 --> 00:00:46,951
winners rarely need miracle breakthroughs,

28
00:00:46,951 --> 00:00:49,320
they simply commit zero unforced errors.
